  "scope": "D3D8 reuses the shared mandatory readback transport. Distinct DEFAULT GPU subresources copy by StretchRect with correct level and cube face, without CPU readback. A private GPU-dirty marker refreshes ordinary texture/cube mirrors before CPU access; render-target locks always refresh, while CPU-accessible pools remain authoritative even after upload failure. Readonly Unlock emits no update; partial writable locks preserve rendered exterior. CPU copies use a snapshot for aliases and upload only dirty rectangles. CPU-only image-to-back-buffer copies stage only the copied rectangle into a transient texture, issue a GPU blit, then destroy that texture. All object/format/depth/lock/range checks precede copy effects; bounded subtraction avoids POINT addition overflow. Format equality and omitted points/zero-count paths follow pinned Wine implementation facts; the locked mip case is pinned by Wine Windows tests. CopyRects preserves existing per-rectangle accepted prefixes. GetFrontBuffer reads the actual proxy back-buffer pixels and retains existing compatible format/pool behavior, preserves a larger CPU destination exterior and makes ARGB opaque. The proxy view is still its output surface, not complete OS desktop/window-offset/front-vs-back presentation semantics. CPU Clear mirror filling, separate swap-chain/COM identity, locked back-buffer behavior, multisample API coverage, cube RT binding (S2-37), wrappers and later stages remain. The real XP probe checks known red/green geometry, a blue partial RT write, independent overlap-gradient data, stale-mirror refresh, CPU upload/cube mip copies, locked mip refusal and rollback after an explicit simulated failed readback. Guest CPU pixel replies are simulated and identified as such. Separate real GPU replay validates all eleven exact requests against independent known inputs over 38912 pixels per backend, including the request whose guest reply was deliberately failed. No original Microsoft HAL or full Wine/game/FPS acceptance is claimed.",
